WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.500
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: With almost
500 sessions at NEXT 2019,

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.750
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.000
So if you didn't see How AI is
Transforming the Enterprise,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.980
then stay tuned, because
here's the rewind.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.226
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.440
AI is no longer science fiction.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.420
It's here, and it's up to
you to be creative with it.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.220
But what does that look
like in practice, and how

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.170
do you actually
use it to transform

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.820
your industry at scale?

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.790
If you want to take a
peek behind that curtain

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.250
and find out how familiar
enterprises adopt

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.910
AI with game-changing results,
then this talk is for you.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.400
It brings you cloud customer
stories of frustrations

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.210
and triumphs-- stories that
are deep, practical, inspiring,

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.500
and very real.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.600
You'll meet Fox Sports,
an Australian media

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.260
and entertainment company that
uses AI to transform sports

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.100
by alerting fans when something
exciting is about to happen.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.870
Join Hulu for a
behind-the-scenes

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.820
look at data quality
and see how AI

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.290
can be used to generate better
data for other AI applications,

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.050
while bringing more joy to the
work of their customer service

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:05.710
agents.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.670
Now, that's
creativity going meta.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.000
Once you've got your creative
vision and your data,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.320
what's next?

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.910
You're going to need a model.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.980
A model is just a
fancy word for recipe.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.160
Now, traditional AI
was all about making

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.510
every recipe yourself.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.560
But what if the model that
you need already exists?

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.470
Why reinvent the
concept of pizza

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.752
when you can use your
favorite chef's pizza recipe?

00:01:29.752 --> 00:01:31.960
Well, it turns out that's
exactly what Google Cloud's

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.280
Machine Learning
APIs are all about.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.060
They are recipes that we have
made for you so you can just

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.300
get cooking.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.790
We've done the machine
learning heavy lifting.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.900
Now you can just plug and play.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.190
Though machine learning
APIs make it easy,

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.430
you can use them to achieve
incredibly visionary things.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.290
You will be blown
away and inspired

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:53.500
to hear about Unilever's
journey with our APIs,

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.040
and you might just raise an
eyebrow at their stunning ROI.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.320
Borrowing existing
solutions is great,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.360
but some industries
are all about creating

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.490
proprietary recipes
from scratch.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.410
Finance is a great example.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.860
You'll hear the world's largest
asset manager, BlackRock, dive

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.900
into how it uses AI to
stay on top of its game

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.100
and navigate its
challenges, including

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.900
the crucial need
for transparency,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.540
the dangers of
unjustified complexity,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.550
and new directions
for their future--

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.470
like natural
language processing.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:26.080
Scale is in Google's
DNA, and it's

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.780
extremely important
to the customers

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.060
you'll meet in this talk.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.030
So scaling up is a huge thing.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.310
But running AI at
global scale doesn't

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.800
mean one size has to fit all.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.830
Disney's story is all
about personalization.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.330
They were one of
the first customers

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.180
to try our Recommendations
AI before its public release

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.380
at Google NEXT.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.230
Now, less than a
year later, they

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.110
are successfully serving a
magical personalized experience

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.630
for users on their
Shop Disney site.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.450
You'll hear insights into their
testing practices, the power

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.680
of anonymized data, and why we
know that Eeyore sleep masks

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.562
really don't go
with Darth Vader.

00:03:01.562 --> 00:03:03.020
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.180
check out the link
in the description

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.730
below, and don't forget to
subscribe to the Cloud YouTube

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.647
channel for more great
NEXT Rewind content.

00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:10.480
Thanks for watching.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

